Linux Basic Commands
I practiced the following basic Linux commands on Ubuntu to understand files, directories, users, processes, system information, and basic system operations.

1. Command:
      pwd
   What it does:
      Shows the current working directory.
   What I learned:
      It tells me where I am currently located in the Linux filesystem.

2. Command:
       ls
   What it does:
       Lists the files and directories in the current directory.
   What I learned:
       I can use it to see what is present in a directory.

3. Command:
       ls -l
   What it does:
       Shows files and directories with detailed information.
   What I learned:
       The output includes permissions, owner, group, size, and modification time.

4. Command:
       cd /var/log
   What it does:
       Changes the current directory.
   What I learned:
       I can use cd to move between directories.

5. Command:
       mkdir test
   What it does:
       Creates a new directory.
   What I learned:
       It is used when I need to create a folder from the terminal.

6. Command:
       touch file.txt
   What it does:
       Creates a new empty file if the file does not already exist.
   What I learned:
       It is a simple way to create a file from the terminal.

7. Command:
       cp file.txt backup.txt
   What it does:
       Copies a file.
   What I learned:
       The original file remains, and a copy is created with the new name.

8. Command:
       mv file.txt /tmp/
   What it does:
       Moves a file or directory to another location.
   What I learned:
       mv can also be used to rename files.

9. Command:
       rm file.txt
   What it does:
       Removes a file.
   What I learned:
       I should be careful when using rm because the file can be deleted from the command line.

10. Command:
        cat file.txt
    What it does:
        Displays the contents of a file in the terminal.
    What I learned:
        It is useful for quickly viewing small text files.

11. Command:
        head file.txt
    What it does:
        Displays the beginning of a file.
    What I learned:
        It is useful when I only want to see the first few lines.

12. Command:
        tail file.txt
    What it does:
        Displays the end of a file.
    What I learned:
        It is useful for checking the latest lines of a file, especially log files.

13. Command:
        clear
    What it does:
        Clears the terminal screen.
    What I learned:
        It makes the terminal easier to read by removing the previous output from the screen.

14. Command:
        whoami
    What it does:
        Shows the username of the current user.
    What I learned:
        It helps me check which user I am currently logged in as.

15. Command:
        id
    What it does:
        Shows the current user's user ID, group ID, and group memberships.
    What I learned:
        It helps me understand the identity and group permissions of the current user.

16. Command:
        uname -a
    What it does:
        Displays detailed information about the Linux system and kernel.
    What I learned:
        It can show information such as the kernel version and system architecture.

17. Command:
        hostname
    What it does:
        Shows the hostname of the system.
    What I learned:
        The hostname identifies the computer on the network.

18. Command:
        history
    What it does:
        Shows previously executed commands.
    What I learned:
        It helps me review commands that I have already run in the terminal.

19. Command:
        sudo <command>
    What it does:
        Runs a command with elevated privileges when the current user is allowed to do so.
    What I learned:
        Some system operations require administrator privileges, so sudo can be used for those commands.
